# Street Fighter 6 (Capcom 2023: RE Engine, DirectX 12 through VKD3D-Proton; Denuvo)
# summary: Denuvo through GameSpark FEX patch 0004 (code checks only where the game rewrites code), DualSense as XInput, Borderless boots unattended
# On stock FEX the game quit about a minute after launch: Denuvo's code faulted with a garbage frame pointer and
# Wine's unwinder overflowed the stack on it. Galactic Racer's cure gets it further: Proton must catch the game's
# direct Windows syscalls (FEX's seccomp emulation plus GameSpark's fix) and x86 hardware execute breakpoints must
# fire, both from GameSpark's patched FEX (system/fex-patched.sh), on a Proton that still has the seccomp syscall trap
# (GameSpark-Proton-Exp-20261008: tools/steam-config.sh compat-tool 1364780 ...).
FEX_BINARY=gamespark
FEX_NEEDSSECCOMP=1
FEX_GSHWBP=1
# Then Denuvo hooks ntdll's NtProtectVirtualMemory stub with WriteProcessMemory (page made copy-on-write, hook bytes
# written, protection restored) and checks the hook. Wine writes those bytes from wineserver (process_vm_writev); with
# FEX's default page tracking (mtrack) FEX had already write-protected the page again because it translated code on
# it, so the write failed: Denuvo raised "Error at hooking API NtProtectVirtualMemory" (a hard error Wine does not
# display) and exited with 0xDEADC0DE 3 s after start. GameSpark's FEX patch 0004 (FEX_GSSMCWX=1) leaves code pages the
# game makes writable unprotected and checks their code before it runs. FEX_SMCCHECKS=full also boots the game, but it
# checks every instruction of the program (48 fps in Training Mode at 4K, 3.5 min to the title).
FEX_GSSMCWX=1
# Borderless and FullScreen mode (the player's choice: Normal mode has a title bar) boot only as far as the health
# warning, then a still frame, until the window is activated again (switching to another window and back);
# Normal mode boots by itself. tools/boot-nudge.py does that switch, through a 1-pixel window, while the screen
# stands still, until the title shows.
BOOT_NUDGE=PRESS ANY BUTTON
# Controllers: Proton presents the DualSense to the game as an XInput pad (PROTON_DISABLE_HIDRAW: through SDL rather
# than as raw HID) and hides Steam's own virtual pads (28de:11ff). The developer opts the game into Steam Input for every
# controller type, so Steam assigns the DualSense an XInput slot (0) for the game. Steam's virtual pads carry no input on
# this machine (the 32-bit Steam client under FEX), so with Proton's defaults (the real pad hidden, Steam's virtual pad
# shown) no pad worked; neither did the real pad as raw HID with no XInput pad.
SteamVirtualGamepadInfo=/dev/null
SteamVirtualGamepadInfo_Proton=/dev/null
SDL_GAMECONTROLLER_IGNORE_DEVICES=0x28de/0x11ff
PROTON_DISABLE_HIDRAW=0x054c/0x0ce6,0x054c/0x0df2
